c Spec 104 fixture — HAND-CONSTRUCTED (hindi_flick is not in this machine's
c partial corpus checkout). It reproduces the idiom attested in the
c spec 104 corpus census (research working papers, corpus-census.md):
c the vowel key keeps its U_ id in the touch layout and the .kmn attaches
c real logic to that exact id — + [U_0905] > 'ा' use(checkMatra).
c U_0906 is deliberately left unruled: it is the U_-decode control.

store(&VERSION) '10.0'
store(&NAME) 'Hindi Flick (spec 104 fixture)'
store(&KEYBOARDVERSION) '1.0'
store(&LAYOUTFILE) 'hindi_flick.keyman-touch-layout'
store(&TARGETS) 'any'

begin Unicode > use(main)

group(main) using keys

+ [U_0905] > 'ा' use(checkMatra)

group(checkMatra) using keys

c Representative matra validation in the source keyboard's group
c shape. A use() group sees the calling rule's output in its context,
c so the first rule fires when the tap would stack a matra sign onto
c an existing one (context U+093E + the main rule's emitted U+093E):
c it keeps one sign and beeps. The catch rule preserves the output
c for every other context — a use() group with NO matching rule lets
c the keystroke fall through to the U_ decode as well (verified by the
c T002 harness), so a governing group must always match.
U+093E U+093E + [U_0905] > U+093E beep
+ [U_0905] > context
